    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Understanding the physical processes responsible for development of off-fault deformation during seismic slip events is important both for our predictive capability of damage zone structure around subsurface faults and for understanding the energy budget of earthquake producing faults. In the past, structural geologists have explained fault-related deformation by utilizing a Lagrangian approach, where characterization of deformation around the fault is used to interpret the fracture kinematics and mechanics of exhumed faults. To this point in time, the vast majority of field-based work has considered only the contribution of the quasi-static stress field to deformation on and near the main fault, ignoring inertial effects resulting from high rupture velocity. The purpose this project is to document a number of fault related structures predicted by considering the dynamic stress field around a propagating rupture, and ultimately to introduce a catalogue of structural features resulting from, and indicative of, high rupture velocities.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
